ude/board.h` | Board-specific macro definitions and function declarations. |\n\n**Chip-level files (if adding new SoC support):**\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `chips/&lt;chip&gt;/Kconfig` | Chip model selection, feature flags |\n| `chips/&lt;chip&gt;/Make.defs` | Lists C source files for compilation |\n| `chips/&lt;chip&gt;/&lt;vendor&gt;_start.c` | Reset entry point (`__start` function): clears BSS, copies .data, initializes clocks/serial, calls `nx_start()` |\n| `chips/&lt;chip&gt;/&lt;vendor&gt;_irq.c` | Interrupt handling |\n| `chips/&lt;chip&gt;/&lt;vendor&gt;_lowputc.c` | Low-level UART output |\n| `chips/&lt;chip&gt;/&lt;vendor&gt;_timeisr.c` | Timer ISR |\n\n### 3. Build System Organization\n\n**How the build system finds boards:**\n\nThe build system uses a `[vendor]-[board]-[config]` triple format. The `dump_build_choices()` function in `/home/warner/openvela/build/envsetup.sh` scans `vendor/*/boards/*/configs/*/` directories looking for both `CMakeLists.txt` and a `configs/` subdirectory.\n\n**Build commands:**\n\n```bash\n# Method 1: Direct build.sh invocation\n./build.sh vendor/openvela/boards/contest2026_XXX_board/configs/nsh --cmake -j8\n\n# Method 2: Using envsetup.sh (recommended for CMake)\nsource build/envsetup.sh\nlunch [openvela]-[contest2026_XXX_board]-[nsh]\nm -j8\n```\n\n**Key defconfig settings** (from the vela/sil examples):\n\n```\nCONFIG_ARCH=\"arm\"                          # or \"riscv\", \"arm64\", etc.\nCONFIG_ARCH_BOARD_CUSTOM=y\nCONFIG_ARCH_BOARD_CUSTOM_DIR=\"../vendor/openvela/boards/&lt;board_dir&gt;/\"\nCONFIG_ARCH_BOARD_CUSTOM_DIR_RELPATH=y\nCONFIG_ARCH_BOARD_CUSTOM_NAME=\"&lt;config_name&gt;\"\nCONFIG_ARCH_CHIP=\"&lt;chip_name&gt;\"\nCONFIG_BOARD_EARLY_INITIALIZE=y\nCONFIG_BOARD_LATE_INITIALIZE=y\nCONFIG_ETC_ROMFS=y\nCONFIG_INIT_ENTRYPOINT=\"nsh_main\"\n```\n\n**Manifest linkage** (from the contest XML):\n\nThe team's `board/contest_board/` directory is symlinked into the build tree via:\n```xml\n&lt;linkfile src=\"board/contest_board\" dest=\"vendor/openvela/boards/contest2026_XXX_board\"/&gt;\n```\n\nThis means the board appears at `vendor/openvela/boards/contest2026_XXX_board/` in the openvela working tree, and the build system discovers it automatically.\n\n### 4. Specific Requirements for the \"新硬件适配\" Track\n\nFrom the contest overview (`/home/warner/openvela/demo/参赛相关/contest_overview (1).md`) and the porting guide (`/home/warner/openvela/docs/zh-cn/chip_porting/porting_guide.md`):\n\n**Track description:** \"将 openvela 移植到全新芯片/开发板，完成 BSP 适配与驱动开发\" (Port openvela to a new chip/development board, complete BSP adaptation and driver development).\n\n**Scoring criteria (100 total):**\n- Technical difficulty (30 pts): New chip adaptation, low-level driver extension, system architecture, deep openvela customization, hardware engineering capability\n- Product innovation (20 pts): Novelty in features/scenarios/AI interaction\n- Project completeness (20 pts): Working code + runnable demo + documentation, stable functionality\n- AI development (10 pts): AI-enabled development workflow, reusable Skills\n- Commercial potential (10 pts): Real application scenarios, scalable\n- Presentation (10 pts): Professional demo and defense\n\n**Minimum deliverables for hardware porting:**\n1. A working BSP with at least `nsh` (NuttShell) booting on the target hardware\n2. UART console driver working (serial output)\n3. Timer driver (arch_alarm or arch_timer model)\n4. Interrupt controller adaptation\n5. Memory/heap initialization\n6. A linker script matching the target hardware's memory map\n\n**The porting guide specifies a 3-layer adaptation model:**\n- Architecture layer (already supported by openvela -- ARM, RISC-V, etc.)\n- Chip/SoC layer (under `vendor/&lt;name&gt;/chips/`)\n- Board layer (under `vendor/&lt;name&gt;/boards/&lt;chip&gt;/&lt;board&gt;/`)\n\n**Build artifacts produced:**\n- `libarch.a` -- architecture static library\n- `libboards.a` -- board driver static library\n- `vela_nuttx.bin` -- final firmware binary for flashing\n\n**Testing:** The guide references an xTS test suite covering kernel, driver BSP, filesystem, WiFi, Bluetooth, audio/video capabilities. Teams should run both \"general self-test cases\" (mandatory: memory, scheduling, GPIO, I2C/SPI, UART, RTC, Watchdog) and \"category self-test cases\" (optional: WiFi, BT, LCD, Audio, FS, OTA).\n\n**Key files referenced:**\n- `/home/warner/openvela/docs/zh-cn/chip_porting/porting_guide.md` -- Full porting guide\n- `/home/warner/openvela/docs/zh-cn/chip_porting/Vendor.md` -- Vendor directory structure\n- `/home/warner/openvela/contest2026_224_quanyuanzhirendui/board/contest_board/` -- Template board skeleton\n- `/home/warner/openvela/vendor/openvela/boards/sil/` -- Complete real board example (QEMU ARM32R)\n- `/home/warner/openvela/vendor/openvela/boards/vela/` -- Another real board example (QEMU goldfish)\n- `/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/tools/build.sh` -- Main build script\n- `/home/warner/openvela/build/envsetup.sh` -- Build environment setup with `lunch`/`m` commands\n- `/home/warner/openvela/vendor/openvela/boards/build/vendorsetup.sh` -- Lunch combo registration</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>80254</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>56</tool_uses><duration_ms>189333</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
